    <body>
      <sec id="article-145266.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Phantosmia, the perception of an odorant in the absence of an identifiable environmental stimulus, is an uncommon yet clinically significant olfactory disorder associated with a broad range of etiologies, including neurologic, psychiatric, sinonasal, and idiopathic causes. Despite its impact on quality of life, phantosmia remains underrecognized and poorly understood in clinical practice, resulting in delayed diagnosis and inconsistent management. This activity provides clinicians with a comprehensive review of the pathophysiology, diagnostic evaluation, and evidence-based treatment strategies for phantosmia. Participants will gain practical skills in differentiating phantosmia from other olfactory disorders, selecting appropriate imaging and diagnostic tools, and implementing etiology-directed treatment plans. The activity also emphasizes the importance of interdisciplinary collaboration and patient-centered care to improve long-term outcomes in this underserved patient population.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the peripheral and central etiologies associated with phantosmia and differentiate phantosmia from other qualitative and quantitative olfactory disorders based on clinical presentation and diagnostic criteria.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine the appropriate diagnostic workup for patients with phantosmia, including the indications for nasal endoscopy, computed tomography of the paranasal sinuses, and magnetic resonance imaging of the brain with attention to the olfactory region.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based, etiology-directed treatment strategies for phantosmia, including antipsychotic, antiseizure, and antimigraine pharmacotherapy, as well as surgical excision of the olfactory mucosa in refractory cases.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interdisciplinary team members, including otolaryngologists, neurologists, psychiatrists, pharmacists, and primary care clinicians, to develop coordinated, patient-centered management plans that address both the olfactory and psychosocial dimensions of phantosmia.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=145266&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=145266">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Phantosmia is a qualitative olfactory disorder wherein an odorant is perceived in the absence of an identifiable stimulus. Phantosmia differs from the closely related qualitative olfactory disorder, parosmia, wherein an erroneous odorant is perceived in response to an identifiable stimulus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r1">[1]</xref> Phantosmia has other names, including olfactory hallucinations or phantom odors. Olfactory disorders are often classified as either conductive or sensorineural. Conductive olfactory dysfunction involves obstruction of an odorant from interacting with the olfactory mucosa. Sensorineural dysfunction results from impaired transmission or processing of olfactory stimuli.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Additionally, classifications such as central and peripheral have been used to describe phantosmia. Peripheral causes of phantosmia are thought to arise from disruption and alteration of the olfactory mucosa and sensory receptors, whereas central phantosmia is believed to originate from aberrant central integration and interpretation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Moreover, phantosmia can adversely affect a patient's quality of life. The sense of smell is a critical component of the enjoyment of life and is intimately involved with taste, social life, and the ability to perceive potential environmental dangers.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Although the exact etiology and pathophysiology of phantosmia are unknown, phantosmia has been described in myriad conditions, including, but not limited to, traumatic head injury, upper respiratory tract infections, aging, temporal lobe seizures, sinusitis, brain tumors, certain medications, systemic diseases such as hypothyroidism, and certain neurocognitive disorders such as Parkinson disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r3">[3]</xref> Other suggested contributing etiologies discussed in the literature include migraine, psychiatric, and mood disorders such as schizophrenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Despite this extensive list, most cases of phantosmia are ultimately idiopathic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r5">[5]</xref> Clinicians may find it helpful to divide phantosmia into 2 major categories: peripheral or central causes. Olfactory dysfunction can also be classified by anatomic location as conductive, sensorineural, or central.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Central etiologies for phantosmia may include traumatic brain injury, aging, temporal lobe seizures, brain tumors, migraines, and neuropsychiatric disorders, including schizophrenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Odorant integration and interpretation occur in multiple brain regions, including the thalamus, hypothalamus, and dorsolateral frontal cortex. Abnormalities in any of these regions may alter olfaction. Sensorineural dysfunction of the olfactory neuroepithelium has been implicated in phantosmia, as described in association with upper respiratory tract infections, including COVID-19.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;The prevailing peripheral phantosmia pathomechanism includes an insult to the neuroepithelium, followed by spurious reinnervation and resultant erroneous olfactory transmission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Phantosmia is a relatively uncommon olfactory disorder, accounting for only 10% to 20% of olfactory presentations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;The prevalence of phantosmia is largely unknown due to many factors, including underestimates with self-reporting, varying testing standards, and population differences.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Olfactory neuroepithelial plasticity varies with age, thereby limiting epidemiologic comparisons. Results from a&#x000a0;recent self-reporting survey of 3606 US adults aged 40 years or older suggested a phantosmia prevalence of 6%, disproportionately affecting women.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from a&#x000a0;similar study among Swedish adults aged 60 years or older suggested a prevalence of 4.9%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Although the association of phantosmia with many disorders, including epilepsy, schizophrenia, Parkinson disease, depression, and migraines, has been well documented, the mechanism behind these associations remains poorly characterized.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The olfactory epithelium is a pseudostratified columnar neuroepithelium at the superior nasal vault, between the septum and middle turbinate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r1">[1]</xref> The basal layer of the olfactory epithelium contains stem cells, which underlie its regenerative capacity. Superficial to the basal layer lie olfactory receptor neurons, which transmit odorant signals through the olfactory nerve, with or without inputs from the trigeminal nerve, to the central nervous system, thereby contributing to odor perception.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Each olfactory neuron expresses a specific G-protein&#x02013;coupled receptor,&#x000a0;which, when&#x000a0;bound by odorants, leads to signal transmission through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Within the olfactory bulb, olfactory nerves of similar receptors converge onto specific glomeruli to synapse with second-order neurons.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r16">[16]</xref> Second-order neurons carry olfactory signals from the olfactory bulb to primary olfactory cortical structures such as the piriform cortex, rostral entorhinal cortex, and periamygdaloid cortex. Further processing and integration occur in the secondary and tertiary olfactory networks, comprising areas including the thalamus, hypothalamus, and dorsolateral frontal cortex.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The history and physical examination for a patient presenting with phantosmia should begin with a full otolaryngologic history and physical examination. Patients with phantosmia&#x000a0;most commonly report spontaneously smelling a&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">burnt</italic>&#x000a0;odor in the absence of an&#x000a0;obvious environmental stimulus, such as actual burning. A burnt smell is the most commonly reported smell associated with phantosmia, but other smells have been reported, including feces, gas, and odors described as rotten, musty, sweet, metallic, or fruity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The history should include the onset and frequency of symptoms, identification of any inciting stimulus, inquiry about how the patient describes the smell, and identification of any exacerbating or relieving factors. Any additional changes to the patient's sense of taste or smell when eating or drinking should also be elicited.&#x000a0;Additionally, the clinician should inquire about the following: history of trauma to the head, psychiatric history, diabetes mellitus, head and neck cancer, migraine, seizure disorders, chronic or recurrent sinusitis or nasal polyps, COVID-19 infection, and Parkinson disease because all of these can have olfactory manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r17">[17]</xref> Obtaining a thorough medication history, including any history of administration of intravenous antibiotics or chemotherapy and radiation therapy, intranasal supplement use (particularly zinc),<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r18">[18]</xref> and a social history including tobacco, alcohol, and recreational drug use, as well as occupational history, is important.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>When evaluating olfactory disorders in general, clinicians should divide them into 2 categories: quantitative and qualitative. Quantitative olfactory disorders include anosmia or hyposmia, in which there is a measurable decrease in the sense of smell, whereas qualitative disorders include parosmia or phantosmia, which are distorted sensations of smell that are more difficult to measure via traditional olfactory testing. Objective and physiologic tests exist to evaluate quantitative olfactory disorders. However, reliable, validated tests for qualitative changes in olfaction are not currently available.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>A thorough history and physical examination remains the starting point in evaluating phantosmia. Nasal endoscopy can be a useful tool for assessing primary nasal pathology, such as chronic sinusitis, nasal polyposis, or tumors. A CT of the paranasal sinuses and an MRI of the brain, with attention to the olfactory region, may also be warranted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>MRI of the brain with intravenous contrast is the modality of choice for examining soft tissues, including the olfactory nerve and nasal mucosal structures, as well as the brain parenchyma. MRI is particularly useful when evaluating for malignant neoplasms, chronic vascular changes, or prior strokes, or neuronal pathology such as multiple sclerosis. CT is the modality of choice for evaluating the bony structures of the skull and is particularly relevant after head trauma. CT can also reveal underlying chronic sinusitis, nasal polyposis, or a sinonasal mass (though not to the same degree of detail as MRI in the case of sinonasal tumors), any of which may contribute to the underlying phantosmia.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Because comparatively little is known about the precise pathophysiology of phantosmia, treatment focuses on identifying the underlying cause. Classifying potential causes into quantitative and qualitative categories can help clinicians organize the workup and investigations, as well as treatment. Various treatment plans for central neurologic causes of phantosmia have been discussed in the literature, including medications such as antipsychotics, anticonvulsants, and antidepressants. For peripheral causes of phantosmia, the goal of treatment would be to inhibit neuroepithelial receptors in the olfactory mucosa from producing a smell in the absence of a stimulus. Methods described in the literature involve applying a local anesthetic such as cocaine to the olfactory mucosa or surgically removing the olfactory mucosa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Results from studies involving local anesthetics (eg, cocaine) placed on the olfactory mucosa have not proven to have long-lasting effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r22">[22]</xref> One study described long-lasting relief of phantosmia after unilateral resection of the olfactory mucosa in a small case series. In this study, 5 patients with phantosmia were treated with haloperidol, and 2 reported symptomatic improvement. The other 3 patients were considered to have unilateral peripheral phantosmia because their symptoms were refractory to the haloperidol trial. Laterality of the defective olfactory epithelium was determined by occluding one nostril for a given amount of time, during which the presence of phantosmia was measured. If phantosmia was experienced with one nostril occluded, the contralateral side was chosen for resection of the olfactory mucosa. All 3 patients who underwent the surgical procedure experienced long-lasting relief of their phantosmia, with relief lasting more than 5 years in 2 patients and 18 months in the third.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Advances in nasal endoscopy have made this method of surgical excision of the olfactory mucosa a safer option; however, risks include cerebrospinal fluid leaks, bleeding, and damage to surrounding structures (eg, orbital contents, orbital nerve, anterior ethmoidal artery, carotid artery, sphenopalatine artery, skull base) associated with this surgical procedure. For these reasons, surgical intervention is considered only after medical treatment has been exhausted and symptoms result in significant impairment of quality of life. Findings from a recent systematic review of phantosmia treatments, including antiseizure medications, antipsychotic medications, antimigraine medications, surgical resection of the olfactory mucosa, and transcranial stimulation, suggested variable etiology-dependent outcomes. For example, most patients with phantosmia during migraine headaches reported resolution of phantosmia with migraine prophylaxis. In patients who underwent olfactory mucosa resection, most experienced symptomatic relief.</p>
        <p>Transcranial stimulation was very effective for short-term relief; however, it failed to provide significant long-term symptom relief. Overall, the findings from the systematic review suggested that in studies involving root-cause directed treatment of phantosmia (ie, antimigraine, antipsychotic, antiseizure, or surgical therapy), 23 of 29 (73.3%) patients experienced long-lasting relief of phantosmia symptoms, compared to only 14 of 44 (31.8%) of patients receiving observation only.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Findings from a 2025 study demonstrated that the use of hyaluronic acid and mint-flavored toothpaste was associated with improved smell and taste scores after multiple weeks of twice-daily use in 16 patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Despite the promising evidence&#x000a0;from these study findings,&#x000a0;a&#x000a0;significant gap remains in establishing robust treatment guidelines. Notably, no randomized controlled studies or head-to-head trials comparing phantosmia treatments exist.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of phantosmia includees the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Parosmia&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyposmia&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anosmia&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Brain mass&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Temporal lobe seizures&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Schizophrenia&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mood disorders&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Upper respiratory tract infection&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nasal or paranasal sinus disease&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic rhinosinusitis&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nasal polyposis&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Migraine&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Epilepsy or other seizure disorder</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurodegenerative disease</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Like other qualitative olfactory disorders, phantosmia is associated with a deterioration of olfactory impairment over time.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r24">[24]</xref> However, compared to other qualitative olfactory disorders (eg, parosmia), phantosmia is associated with a faster rate of spontaneous recovery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Although many treatments for phantosmia have been proposed, most have variable effectiveness and limited evidence of improved outcomes. Traditionally, the etiology of phantosmia was considered a key determinant of prognosis, with certain etiologies (eg, traumatic injury) associated with a worse prognosis than others (eg, upper respiratory tract infection). This association, however, is becoming less clear.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Improved prognosis is increasingly associated with sex, smoking status, and disease severity, with young individuals who do not smoke but have severe disease carrying an improved prognosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r26">[26]</xref> The improved prognosis in these groups may be associated with a greater regenerative capacity of the neuroepithelium.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Addressing the underlying cause of phantosmia remains the cornerstone of phantosmia management, after which resolution of olfactory impairments is often achieved.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Although the practical complications of phantosmia are less pronounced, phantosmia may lead to severe adverse outcomes, including social isolation and anhedonia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Depression, anxiety, and other mood-related disorders may develop due to this debilitating disease in some patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-145266.r29">[29]</xref> An empathic interdisciplinary team approach involving psychiatrists, otolaryngologists, and others is encouraged for all patients. Complications may also arise from treatment attempts. Antipsychotics, antimigraine medications, and antiseizure medications are not without adverse effects.</p>
        <p>Finally, as a last resort, surgical intervention with endoscopic excision of olfactory epithelium involves known risks of cerebrospinal fluid leak, skull base injury, orbital injury, bleeding, and more. Risk versus benefit analysis should always be discussed with the patient before undergoing treatment. These risks may be&#x000a0;exacerbated by clinicians' frequent trivialization of symptoms. Olfactory deficits merit similar attention as visual and auditory deficits and should be addressed appropriately to avoid adverse patient outcomes and foster trust within the patient-clinician relationship.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Mitigating the effects of phantosmia can be difficult due to the lack of treatment options. The debilitating effects of phantosmia may be mitigated by addressing underlying etiologies and employing patient education. This approach is supported by many cases of phantosmia secondary to migraines, seizures, or schizophrenia resolving with treatment of the underlying etiology.</p>
        <p>The umbrella of olfactory disorders is broad, and educating patients about their particular disorder can help them differentiate phantosmia from other olfactory disorders. This differentiation can help further guide evaluation and treatment. Understanding the underlying etiology of a patient's phantosmia helps the patient pursue further treatment and potentially alleviate symptoms. Phantosmia can be an extremely debilitating disease and severely affect a patient's quality of life. Consequently, clinicians should understand the definition of phantosmia and its etiology, evaluation, and treatment to educate patients effectively.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s13" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Clinical pearls regarding phantosmia include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Phantosmia describes the perception of an odorant in the absence of an identifiable stimulus in the environment.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Classifications of olfactory disorders include conductive, sensorineural, peripheral, and central.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A myriad of etiologies exists for phantosmia. However, idiopathic causes are the most common.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A burnt smell is the most commonly reported smell associated with phantosmia, but other smells have been reported, such as feces, an odor characterized as rotten, musty, gas, sweet, metallic, or fruity.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>When history and physical examination are unrevealing for an obvious root cause of phantosmia, imaging with CT&#x000a0;of the paranasal sinuses&#x000a0;or MRI&#x000a0;of the&#x000a0;brain, with attention to the olfactory region,&#x000a0;may be helpful.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Treatment is etiology-dependent, and typically medical therapies (ie, antipsychotics, anticonvulsants, antimigraine medications) are exhausted before attempting endoscopic surgical excision of olfactory mucosa.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Overall, little is known about phantosmia and its treatment. Further studies should focus on characterizing pathomechanisms and treatment strategies.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-145266.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>An interdisciplinary, team-based approach is always favored when caring for patients with phantosmia. Often, patients with phantosmia may see several clinicians,&#x000a0;including their primary care clinician, otolaryngologist, psychiatrist, neurosurgeon, and neurologist. Pharmacists, radiologists, nurses, social workers, and several other health care professionals may be involved in the care of patients with phantosmia. Primary care clinicians will often play an important role in first identifying and diagnosing phantosmia as well as proper initial workup and referral to other specialists. Consequently, all clinicians should recognize the initial signs and symptoms of patients with phantosmia as early as possible.</p>
        <p>Otolaryngologists play an important role in diagnosing phantosmia and treating many of its root causes. If a patient presents with phantosmia and is found to have chronic sinusitis, nasal polyps, a sinonasal mass, a skull base mass, or other disease processes that may affect the olfactory epithelium, the otolaryngologist should perform a thorough workup&#x000a0;with nasal endoscopy&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;imaging studies and then provide&#x000a0;appropriate treatment. Often, otolaryngologists will surgically treat sinonasal disease or work in conjunction with neurosurgery to treat certain skull base masses. Otolaryngologists also perform the endoscopic surgical excision of olfactory epithelium for patients whose symptoms are refractory to medical therapy and who seek that option.</p>
        <p>Neurologists play an important role in treating many central causes of phantosmia, such as migraines, neurodegenerative disorders such as Alzheimer disease or Parkinson disease, and temporal lobe seizures. Psychiatry consultation is indicated in most patients with phantosmia, given the high prevalence of schizophrenia and other mood disorders in this population. In summary, a team-based approach is imperative to improve long-term outcomes for patients with phantosmia.</p>
      </sec>
